From 0d424f1a5df7fcaa1fc7551570b9ee581c06365f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:09:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] Big screen: Microsoft Teams meetings, opened and
 switched from the control board
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Control board → Sources → Teams meeting: paste the invitation, a join link
or the meeting ID (+ passcode); "Put it on the big screen". Live check of
what was understood (passcode masked), work/school vs personal detected,
optional name, recent meetings one tap away (this browser only).

Teams can't run inside another page (Microsoft forbids framing), so the
projector opens the meeting in its own window, sized to the screen. Found in
testing: once that window lands on an isolating site the browser cuts it off
from the opener — the projector can then neither see nor close it (it looked
"closed" after a second). So after joining, one click on the projector —
"Show it inside the big screen" — captures the Teams window as a big-screen
scene. From then on the board has Show Teams / Back to Knock Knock (meeting
keeps running, sound too) / End meeting; before that it honestly offers
"End & back to Knock Knock" with the projector steps.

- screenart/teams.py: parse() — pasted invitations (Meeting ID / Passcode),
  meetup-join / meet / teams.live.com / teams.cloud.microsoft links, Outlook
  Safe Links unwrapped; only real Teams hosts are ever opened
- state on / shown / mode ("window" | "inside", reported by the projector)
- POST /screenart/s/<token>/teams/ (check / open / show / hide / close /
  report); "teams" in the screen state; the scene uses the screen transitions
- pop-ups blocked → one "Open the meeting" button + how to allow them
